blocks.text:
&ldquo;Pray keep your seat, madam, I will not disturb you.&nbsp; There&mdash;now it
            is pretty well concealed; one would hardly know it was there.&nbsp; Can I see your husband a moment,
            madam?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, Mary, and that is everything.&nbsp; Don&rsquo;t mind
            my talk&mdash;it&rsquo;s just a moment&rsquo;s irritation and doesn&rsquo;t
            mean anything.&nbsp; Kiss me&mdash;there, it&rsquo;s all gone now, and
            I am not complaining any more.&nbsp; What have you been getting?&nbsp;
            What&rsquo;s in the sack?&rdquo;

&ldquo;It weighs a hundred and sixty pounds?&nbsp; Why, Mary, it&rsquo;s
            for-ty thou-sand dollars&mdash;think of it&mdash;a whole fortune!&nbsp;
            Not ten men in this village are worth that much.&nbsp; Give me the paper.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Everybody will grant that, Edward&mdash;grant it privately,
            anyway.&nbsp; For six months, now, the village has been its own proper
            self once more&mdash;honest, narrow, self-righteous, and stingy.&rdquo;

&ldquo;He is not a bad man.&nbsp; I know.&nbsp; The whole of his
            unpopularity had its foundation in that one thing&mdash;the thing that
            made so much noise.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It is a confession.&nbsp; I am ashamed, but I will make it.&nbsp;
            I was the only man who knew he was innocent.&nbsp; I could have saved
            him, and&mdash;and&mdash;well, you know how the town was wrought up&mdash;I
            hadn&rsquo;t the pluck to do it.&nbsp; It would have turned everybody
            against me.&nbsp; I felt mean, ever so mean; but I didn&rsquo;t dare;
            I hadn&rsquo;t the manliness to face that.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&mdash;I don&rsquo;t think it would have done for you to&mdash;to&mdash;One
            mustn&rsquo;t&mdash;er&mdash;public opinion&mdash;one has to be so careful&mdash;so&mdash;&rdquo;&nbsp;
            It was a difficult road, and she got mired; but after a little she got
            started again.&nbsp; &ldquo;It was a great pity, but&mdash;Why, we couldn&rsquo;t
            afford it, Edward&mdash;we couldn&rsquo;t indeed.&nbsp; Oh, I wouldn&rsquo;t
            have had you do it for anything!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh,&rdquo; exclaimed the wife, in a tone of relief, &ldquo;I
            am glad of that.&nbsp; As long as he doesn&rsquo;t know that you could
            have saved him, he&mdash;he&mdash;well that makes it a great deal better.&nbsp;
            Why, I might have known he didn&rsquo;t know, because he is always trying
            to be friendly with us, as little encouragement as we give him.&nbsp;
            More than once people have twitted me with it.&nbsp; There&rsquo;s the
            Wilsons, and the Wilcoxes, and the Harknesses, they take a mean pleasure
            in saying &lsquo;<i>Your friend</i> Burgess,&rsquo; because they know
            it pesters me.&nbsp; I wish he wouldn&rsquo;t persist in liking us so;
            I can&rsquo;t think why he keeps it up.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I can explain it.&nbsp; It&rsquo;s another confession.&nbsp;
            When the thing was new and hot, and the town made a plan to ride him
            on a rail, my conscience hurt me so that I couldn&rsquo;t stand it,
            and I went privately and gave him notice, and he got out of the town
            and stayed out till it was safe to come back.&rdquo;

&ldquo;So do I, now, for it would have been a dreadful way to treat
            him.&nbsp; Yes, I&rsquo;m glad; for really you did owe him that, you
            know.&nbsp; But, Edward, suppose it should come out yet, some day!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Just like Goodson; it&rsquo;s got all the marks.&nbsp; He
            had only one vanity; he thought he could give advice better than any
            other person.&rdquo;

Then they took up the gold-sack mystery again, with strong interest.&nbsp;
            Soon the conversation began to suffer breaks&mdash;interruptions caused
            by absorbed thinkings.&nbsp; The breaks grew more and more frequent.&nbsp;
            At last Richards lost himself wholly in thought.&nbsp; He sat long,
            gazing vacantly at the floor, and by-and-by he began to punctuate his
            thoughts with little nervous movements of his hands that seemed to indicate
            vexation.&nbsp; Meantime his wife too had relapsed into a thoughtful
            silence, and her movements were beginning to show a troubled discomfort.&nbsp;
            Finally Richards got up and strode aimlessly about the room, ploughing
            his hands through his hair, much as a somnambulist might do who was
            having a bad dream.&nbsp; Then he seemed to arrive at a definite purpose;
            and without a word he put on his hat and passed quickly out of the house.&nbsp;
            His wife sat brooding, with a drawn face, and did not seem to be aware
            that she was alone.&nbsp; Now and then she murmured, &ldquo;Lead us
            not into t ... but&mdash;but&mdash;we are so poor, so poor! ...
            Lead us not into.... Ah, who would be hurt by it?&mdash;and no one
            would ever know.... Lead us.... &rdquo;&nbsp; The voice died out
            in mumblings.&nbsp; After a little she glanced up and muttered in a
            half-frightened, half-glad way&mdash;

&ldquo;He is gone!&nbsp; But, oh dear, he may be too late&mdash;too
            late.... Maybe not&mdash;maybe there is still time.&rdquo;&nbsp; She
            rose and stood thinking, nervously clasping and unclasping her hands.&nbsp;
            A slight shudder shook her frame, and she said, out of a dry throat,
            &ldquo;God forgive me&mdash;it&rsquo;s awful to think such things&mdash;but.... Lord, how we are
            made&mdash;how strangely we are made!&rdquo;

She turned the light low, and slipped stealthily over and knelt down
            by the sack and felt of its ridgy sides with her hands, and fondled
            them lovingly; and there was a gloating light in her poor old eyes.&nbsp;
            She fell into fits of absence; and came half out of them at times to
            mutter &ldquo;If we had only waited!&mdash;oh, if we had only waited
            a little, and not been in such a hurry!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, sir.&nbsp; Time-table for Brixton and all the towns beyond
            changed to-day, sir&mdash;had to get the papers in twenty minutes earlier
            than common.&nbsp; I had to rush; if I had been two minutes later&mdash;&rdquo;

The men turned and walked slowly away, not waiting to hear the rest.&nbsp;
            Neither of them spoke during ten minutes; then Cox said, in a vexed
            tone,

&ldquo;If you had only waited, Edward&mdash;if you had only stopped
            to think; but no, you must run straight to the printing-office and spread
            it all over the world.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, yes&mdash;yes, it is true; but when I thought what a
            stir it would make, and what a compliment it was to Hadleyburg that
            a stranger should trust it so&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&mdash;Well, Mary, I feel a good deal as you do: I certainly
            do.&nbsp; It seems strange, too, so strange.&nbsp; I never could have
            believed it&mdash;never.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&rsquo;m past it.&nbsp; Let us make a pallet here; we&rsquo;ve
            got to stand watch till the bank vault opens in the morning and admits
            the sack... Oh dear, oh dear&mdash;if we hadn&rsquo;t made the mistake!&rdquo;

&ldquo;The open sesame&mdash;what could it have been?&nbsp; I do
            wonder what that remark could have been.&nbsp; But come; we will get
            to bed now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, Edward, how lucky it was you did him that grand service,
            poor Goodson!&nbsp; I never liked him, but I love him now.&nbsp; And
            it was fine and beautiful of you never to mention it or brag about it.&rdquo;&nbsp;
            Then, with a touch of reproach, &ldquo;But you ought to have told <i>me</i>,
            Edward, you ought to have told your wife, you know.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now stop hemming and hawing, and tell me about it, Edward.&nbsp;
            I always loved you, and now I&rsquo;m proud of you.&nbsp; Everybody
            believes there was only one good generous soul in this village, and
            now it turns out that you&mdash;Edward, why don&rsquo;t you tell me?&rdquo;

&ldquo;No ... no.&nbsp; We have wandered far enough from our bearings&mdash;God
            spare us that!&nbsp; In all your life you have never uttered a lie.&nbsp;
            But now&mdash;now that the foundations of things seem to be crumbling
            from under us, we&mdash;we&mdash;&rdquo;&nbsp; She lost her voice for
            a moment, then said, brokenly, &ldquo;Lead us not into temptation. .
            . I think you made the promise, Edward.&nbsp; Let it rest so.&nbsp;
            Let us keep away from that ground.&nbsp; Now&mdash;that is all gone
            by; let us he happy again; it is no time for clouds.&rdquo;

The Wilsons devised a grand new thing&mdash;a fancy-dress ball.&nbsp;
            They made no actual promises, but told all their acquaintanceship in
            confidence that they were thinking the matter over and thought they
            should give it&mdash;&ldquo;and if we do, you will be invited, of course.&rdquo;&nbsp;
            People were surprised, and said, one to another, &ldquo;Why, they are
            crazy, those poor Wilsons, they can&rsquo;t afford it.&rdquo;&nbsp;
            Several among the nineteen said privately to their husbands, &ldquo;It
            is a good idea, we will keep still till their cheap thing is over, then
            <i>we</i> will give one that will make it sick.&rdquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;The remark which I made to the distressed stranger
            was this: &ldquo;You are very far from being a bad man; go, and reform.&rdquo;&rsquo;&rdquo;&nbsp;
            Then he continued:&mdash;&ldquo;We shall know in a moment now whether
            the remark here quoted corresponds with the one concealed in the sack;
            and if that shall prove to be so&mdash;and it undoubtedly will&mdash;this
            sack of gold belongs to a fellow-citizen who will henceforth stand before
            the nation as the symbol of the special virtue which has made our town
            famous throughout the land&mdash;Mr. Billson!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Purloined!&rdquo; retorted Billson.&nbsp; &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll
            let you know that neither you nor any man of your kidney must venture
            to&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Mr. Chairman, if I may be permitted to make a suggestion,
            can both of these gentlemen be right?&nbsp; I put it to you, sir, can
            both have happened to say the very same words to the stranger?&nbsp;
            It seems to me&mdash;&rdquo;

The Chair.&nbsp; &ldquo;Order!&nbsp; Sit down, if you please&mdash;both
            of you.&nbsp; Neither of the notes has been out of my possession at
            any moment.&rdquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;<i>Go, and reform&mdash;or, mark my words&mdash;some
                day, for your sins you will die and go to hell or Hadleyburg</i>&mdash;TRY
            AND MAKE IT THE FORMER.&rsquo;&rdquo;

Billson was not used to emergencies; he sat in a helpless collapse.&nbsp;
            But Wilson was a lawyer.&nbsp; He struggled to his feet, pale and worried,
            and said:

&ldquo;&lsquo;The remark which I made to the stranger&mdash;[Voices.&nbsp;
            &ldquo;Hello! how&rsquo;s this?&rdquo;]&mdash;was this: &lsquo;You are
            far from being a bad man.&nbsp; [Voices.&nbsp; &ldquo;Great Scott!&rdquo;]&nbsp;
            Go, and reform.&rsquo;&rdquo;&nbsp; [Voice.&nbsp; &ldquo;Oh, saw my
            leg off!&rdquo;]&nbsp; Signed by Mr. Pinkerton the banker.&rdquo;

The Chair [reading].&nbsp; &ldquo;&lsquo;The remark which I made,&rsquo;
            etc.&nbsp; &lsquo;You are far from being a bad man.&nbsp; Go,&rsquo;
            etc.&nbsp; Signed, &lsquo;Gregory Yates.&rsquo;&rdquo;

The Chair fished again, and once more the familiar words began to
            fall from its lips&mdash;&ldquo;&lsquo;You are far from being a bad
            man&mdash;&rsquo;&rdquo;

Somebody wailed in, and began to sing this rhyme (leaving out &ldquo;it&rsquo;s&rdquo;)
            to the lovely &ldquo;Mikado&rdquo; tune of &ldquo;When a man&rsquo;s
            afraid of a beautiful maid;&rdquo; the audience joined in, with joy;
            then, just in time, somebody contributed another line&mdash;

&ldquo;Perhaps they all contain the secret.&nbsp; I move that you
            open them all and read every signature that is attached to a note of
            that sort&mdash;and read also the first eight words of the note.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That&rsquo;s the hall-marked truth, too!&nbsp; If the Chair
            is right, let the house speak up and say it.&nbsp; Rise!&nbsp; Now,
            then&mdash;hip! hip! hip!&mdash;all together!&rdquo;

Many voices.&nbsp; &ldquo;Right!&mdash;the Chair is right&mdash;no
            interruption can be permitted at this stage!&nbsp; Go on!&mdash;the
            names! the names!&mdash;according to the terms of the motion!&rdquo;

The house burst out with its &ldquo;Mikado&rdquo; travesty, and sang
            it three times with ever-increasing enthusiasm, rising to its feet when
            it reached for the third time the closing line&mdash;

Then Wingate, the saddler, got up and proposed cheers &ldquo;for
            the cleanest man in town, the one solitary important citizen in it who
            didn&rsquo;t try to steal that money&mdash;Edward Richards.&rdquo;

The Tanner (with bitter sarcasm).&nbsp; &ldquo;That&rsquo;s easy.&nbsp;
            The money has to be divided among the eighteen Incorruptibles.&nbsp;
            They gave the suffering stranger twenty dollars apiece&mdash;and that
            remark&mdash;each in his turn&mdash;it took twenty-two minutes for the
            procession to move past.&nbsp; Staked the stranger&mdash;total contribution,
            $360.&nbsp; All they want is just the loan back&mdash;and interest&mdash;forty
            thousand dollars altogether.&rdquo;

A Cyclone of Voices.&nbsp; &ldquo;Open it!&nbsp; Open it!&nbsp; The
            Eighteen to the front!&nbsp; Committee on Propagation of the Tradition!&nbsp;
            Forward&mdash;the Incorruptibles!&rdquo;

&ldquo;By right of apparent seniority in this business, Mr. Wilson
            is Chairman of the Committee on Propagation of the Tradition.&nbsp;
            I suggest that he step forward on behalf of his pals, and receive in
            trust the money.&rdquo;

They sat down, and all the Symbols except &ldquo;Dr.&rdquo; Clay
            Harkness got up, violently protesting against the proposed outrage,
            and threatening to&mdash;

&ldquo;All right, I&rsquo;ll give it.&nbsp; I will come to the hotel
            at ten in the morning.&nbsp; I don&rsquo;t want it known; will see you
            privately.&rdquo;

&ldquo;At nine I will call for the sack, and at eleven will deliver
            the rest of the ten thousand to Mr. Richards in person at his home.&nbsp;
            Good-night.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Put them in the fire! quick! we mustn&rsquo;t be tempted.&nbsp;
            It is a trick to make the world laugh at <i>us</i>, along with the rest,
            and&mdash;Give them to <i>me</i>, since you can&rsquo;t do it!&rdquo;&nbsp;
            He snatched them and tried to hold his grip till he could get to the
            stove; but he was human, he was a cashier, and he stopped a moment to
            make sure of the signature.&nbsp; Then he came near to fainting.

&ldquo;Look here&mdash;look at this!&nbsp;
            Fifteen&mdash;fifteen&mdash;fifteen&mdash;thirty-four.&nbsp;
            Thirty-eight thousand five hundred!&nbsp; Mary, the sack isn&rsquo;t
            worth twelve dollars, and Harkness&mdash;apparently&mdash;has paid about
            par for it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, it looks like it.&nbsp; And the cheques are made to
            &lsquo;Bearer,&rsquo;
            too.&rdquo;

&ldquo;A hint to collect them at some distant bank, I reckon.&nbsp;
            Perhaps Harkness doesn&rsquo;t want the matter known.&nbsp; What is
            that&mdash;a note?&rdquo;

&ldquo;If those beautiful words were deserved, Mary&mdash;and God
            knows I believed I deserved them once&mdash;I think I could give the
            forty thousand dollars for them.&nbsp; And I would put that paper away,
            as representing more than gold and jewels, and keep it always.&nbsp;
            But now&mdash;We could not live in the shadow of its accusing presence,
            Mary.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Saved, once more.&nbsp; And on such terms!&rdquo;&nbsp; He
            put the note in the fire.&nbsp; &ldquo;I&mdash;I wish I were dead, Mary,
            I wish I were out of it all!&rdquo;

Six days passed, then came more news.&nbsp; The old couple were dying.&nbsp;
            Richards&rsquo;s mind cleared in his latest hour, and he sent for Burgess.&nbsp;
            Burgess said:

&mdash;&ldquo;And then he did a natural and justifiable thing; he
            repented of the saving kindness which he had done me, and he <i>exposed</i>
            me&mdash;as I deserved&mdash;&rdquo;

The last of the sacred Nineteen had fallen a prey to the fiendish
            sack; the town was stripped of the last rag of its ancient glory.&nbsp;
            Its mourning was not showy, but it was deep.
